{
  "sn3.19:0.1": "Linked Discourses 3.19 ",
  "sn3.19:0.2": "2. Childless ",
  "sn3.19:0.3": "Childless (1st) ",
  "sn3.19:1.1": "At Sāvatthī. ",
  "sn3.19:1.2": "Then King Pasenadi of Kosala went up to the Buddha in the middle of the day, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him, ",
  "sn3.19:1.3": "“So, Great King, where are you coming from in the middle of the day?” ",
  "sn3.19:2.1": "“Sir, here in Sāvatthī a financier householder has passed away. ",
  "sn3.19:2.2": "Since he died childless, I have come after transferring his fortune to the royal compound. ",
  "sn3.19:2.3": "There was eight million in gold coin, not to mention the silver coin. ",
  "sn3.19:2.4": "And yet that financier ate meals of ",
  "sn3.19:2.5": "sour gruel with false black pepper. ",
  "sn3.19:2.6": "He wore clothes consisting of ",
  "sn3.19:2.7": "three patches of sunn hemp. ",
  "sn3.19:2.8": "He traveled around in a vehicle that was ",
  "sn3.19:2.9": "a dilapidated little cart, holding a leaf as parasol.” ",
  "sn3.19:3.1": "“That’s so true, Great King! That’s so true! ",
  "sn3.19:3.2": "When an untrue person has acquired notable wealth they don’t make themselves happy and pleased. Nor do they make their mother and father, partners and children, bondservants, workers, and staff, and friends and colleagues happy and pleased. And they don’t establish an uplifting religious donation for ascetics and brahmins that’s conducive to heaven, ripens in happiness, and leads to heaven. ",
  "sn3.19:3